    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# The Paradox\n",
    "\n",
    "Every year, the Australian Bureau of Statistics releases its *Recorded Crime* report. Every year, the headline is the same: crime is down. Yet walk down any street and ask Australians how safe they feel, and a different story emerges.\n",
    "\n",
    "This report investigates one of the most persistent puzzles in Australian public life: **the divergence between objective safety and subjective fear**. We bring together three decades of crime data, national surveys on social cohesion, and emerging research on the psychology of risk perception to understand why the numbers and the nerves no longer align.\n",
    "\n",
    "> \"The safest generation of Australians in recorded history is also the most anxious about crime.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "### What this report covers\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **The data story** — How crime has actually changed in Australia since 1990\n",
    "2. **The perception gap** — What surveys tell us about how safe Australians *feel*\n",
    "3. **The media lens** — The role of news coverage and social media in shaping fear\n",
    "4. **The urban factor** — How city design, nightlife, and public space influence safety\n",
    "5. **The policy question** — What governments can do when the problem is perception\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# The Data: What the Numbers Actually Say\n",
    "\n",
    "Before we can explain the gap between reality and perception, we must first establish what reality looks like. The data tells a remarkably consistent story.\n",
    "\n",
